NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"source-delete-for-a-key-the-source-never-held","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source delete for a key the source never held"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"06cab6fc7"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" after #2005). Tested revision:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"74e5b80d6"},{"type":"text","value":". Production code is unchanged from the base revision."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A source may delete a key it does not hold. A typical cause: the backend\naccepted an optimistic insert, then deleted the row before the source\nstreamed it. The maintainer decision for this owner is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An accepted optimistic snapshot of the key retires, as it does for any\nordinary source publication. The row disappears."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An active request for the key stays in place. The delete removes no base\nrow and acknowledges no request."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" already behaves this way. This review adds the generated histories\nand witnesses. It changes no production code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gap","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coverage map listed the case as open. The optimistic-history driver\ndropped every delete for a key the source did not hold before the model or\nthe Collection saw it. So no optimistic-history campaign ever wrote such a\ndelete."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"witnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Witnesses"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The driver keeps deletes for keys the source does not hold and counts them.\nThe fixed campaign must write at least one, beside deletes of held keys."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three pinned histories in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" accept an optimistic\ninsert and then delete its key from a source that never held it. They cover\na queued delete, an immediate delete, and a delete written inside the\ninsert handler."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A fourth pinned history writes an immediate delete while the insert is\nstill active. The row stays visible through settlement."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC outcomes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record claims a repaired generated-history grammar, so ORC-012 applies.\nEach requirement follows."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 contract authority and limits: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The maintainer decision\nabove is the authority. The oracle's opening prose states the contract,\nand Limits below states what it does not claim."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 independent judgment: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model applies its existing drain\nrule. Every ordinary source publication retires accepted snapshots, and a\nsource delete acknowledges no request. The model reads no production state\nand imports no production classifier."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 distinguishable responsibilities: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic-history-oracle.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the contract prose, the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HistoryModel"},{"type":"text","value":", the driver "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runOptimisticHistory"},{"type":"text","value":", and its per-step refinement\ncheck apart. The grammar lives in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceBatch"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimisticHistory"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 grammar controls: met, with one gap."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconstruction: the four pinned histories are legal histories of the\ngrammar. Edits and source deletes both draw keys from 1 to 3."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ablation and range: the fixed-campaign control requires absent-key\ndeletes, and it requires more deletes in total than absent-key deletes, so\nheld-key deletes remain."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exclusion: the driver no longer filters deletes. The source still writes\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insert"},{"type":"text","value":" only for a key it lacks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap: no counter shows that the generator reaches an absent-key delete\nwhile an accepted snapshot of the same key exists. The pinned histories\nreach that premise directly."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 production path and observation: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The driver writes through\nthe real sync "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"begin"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"write"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":" API of a Collection. After each\nstep it compares public rows, both subscribers' replicas, downstream query\nrows, and request outcomes with the model."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 checker calibration: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A hostile mutant rejects a sync delete\nfor a key absent from the source projection. With the old grammar, the\noptimistic-history campaigns pass under it. Only the separate retained\nauthoritative state property fails, and that property has no optimistic\nlayers. With the new grammar, the fixed and random optimistic-history\ncampaigns and the three accepted-insert histories fail. This ran on the\ntree of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"af0b8648a"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"74e5b80d6"},{"type":"text","value":" adds only the fourth pinned history. A\nmutant that drops any delete for a key absent from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncedData"},{"type":"text","value":" is too broad\nto distinguish the change. The old grammar already kills it through queued\ndeletes of held keys."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 fixed and random campaigns: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The fixed campaign uses seed\n86103. The random campaign runs under the property name\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-state.optimistic-history"},{"type":"text","value":", which accepts a seed and path for\nreplay. Both pass on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"74e5b80d6"},{"type":"text","value":", and so do the 153 tests in the four files\nthat use the driver. On "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"af0b8648a"},{"type":"text","value":", the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" suite passed 244\nfiles and 8,269 tests."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 stateful-model minimality: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model gains no\nstate and loses none. An absent-key delete takes the existing delete branch\nof the drain."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 vocabulary mapping: met, with a recorded term."}]},{"type":"text","value":" \"Accepted\nsnapshot\" maps to a completed direct mutation that the Collection retains\nuntil source publication. The model's existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retired"},{"type":"text","value":" flag means that\nthe snapshot stopped overlaying the base. That differs from the glossary's\ndemand retirement. This change keeps the existing model term and does not\nrename it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 failure fidelity and cleanup: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" This change alters\nno shrinking, capture, or cleanup path. The driver keeps its existing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withHistoryCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 independent second formulation: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No reviewer\nnamed a fault that production and the model could share for this case."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 review evidence: met by this record."}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013 distinguishing witness: met, with one limit."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The law is\nconditional: an accepted snapshot retires, but an active request stays. The\naccepted-insert histories reach the first side. The active-insert history is\nthe nearby witness for the second side. A wrong design that also removed the\nactive row would differ from the model at the step after the delete. No\nmutant demonstrates that rejection."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014 controlled-premise handoff: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The oracle uses no\ncontrolled provider or host. The source is the Collection's own sync API."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An immediate delete that arrives while the insert is active leaves the\naccepted row until the next source publication. That follows the existing\nrule that a delete acknowledges no request. Immediate sync batches are\nscheduled for removal in a separate change, so this record does not revisit\nthat rule."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"update-2026-10-05-settlement-drop-law","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update, 2026-10-05: settlement-drop law"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record above describes the retention law that was current on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"06cab6fc7"},{"type":"text","value":". The settlement-drop change replaces that law. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the settlement-drop review"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The following\nnow applies to this owner:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A source delete for a key the source never held removes no applied synced\nrow and acknowledges no request."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the source commits the delete while the transaction persists, or inside\nits handler, the delete is queued. The completed row is held, and the drop\nand the delete publish together. The row is gone after settlement."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once the optimistic state has dropped, the delete changes no visible row."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A persisting transaction keeps its optimistic row. This includes a truncate\nthat carries the delete: the truncate applies at once, beneath that row."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"immediate"},{"type":"text","value":" dimension is gone, because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"begin({ immediate })"},{"type":"text","value":" was\nremoved."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The pinned histories in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"\nnow cover these cases:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a queued delete;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a delete inside the handler;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a delete after the drop;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a persisting insert with a queued delete;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a persisting insert with a truncate delete."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The driver and its counters are unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutants on the merged branch:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reject (throw on) a delete for a key absent from the source projection:"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nkilled. The state-retention oracle fails 11 tests, including the fixed and\nrandom campaigns and the pinned histories."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Silently drop the same delete:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" survived, and is equivalent under the\nnew law. The key is absent from the applied synced rows, and the\noptimistic state drops at settlement whether or not a queued sync\ntransaction touches the key. So readers see the same rows and the same\npublications. Under the retention law, this delete was what retired the\naccepted snapshot, so the mutant was observable there."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ORC-004 gap and the ORC-013 limit recorded above still apply."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"source-delete-for-a-key-the-source-never-held","text":"Source delete for a key the source never held","level":1},{"id":"contract","text":"Contract","level":2},{"id":"gap","text":"Gap","level":2},{"id":"witnesses","text":"Witnesses","level":2},{"id":"orc-outcomes","text":"ORC outcomes","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2},{"id":"update-2026-10-05-settlement-drop-law","text":"Update, 2026-10-05: settlement-drop law","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Source delete for a key the source never held\n\nBase revision: `06cab6fc7` (`main` after #2005). Tested revision:\n`74e5b80d6`. Production code is unchanged from the base revision.\n\n## Contract\n\nA source may delete a key it does not hold. A typical cause: the backend\naccepted an optimistic insert, then deleted the row before the source\nstreamed it. The maintainer decision for this owner is:\n\n- An accepted optimistic snapshot of the key retires, as it does for any\n  ordinary source publication. The row disappears.\n- An active request for the key stays in place. The delete removes no base\n  row and acknowledges no request.\n\n`main` already behaves this way. This review adds the generated histories\nand witnesses. It changes no production code.\n\n## Gap\n\nThe coverage map listed the case as open. The optimistic-history driver\ndropped every delete for a key the source did not hold before the model or\nthe Collection saw it. So no optimistic-history campaign ever wrote such a\ndelete.\n\n## Witnesses\n\n- The driver keeps deletes for keys the source does not hold and counts them.\n  The fixed campaign must write at least one, beside deletes of held keys.\n- Three pinned histories in\n  `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts` accept an optimistic\n  insert and then delete its key from a source that never held it. They cover\n  a queued delete, an immediate delete, and a delete written inside the\n  insert handler.\n- A fourth pinned history writes an immediate delete while the insert is\n  still active. The row stays visible through settlement.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\nThe record claims a repaired generated-history grammar, so ORC-012 applies.\nEach requirement follows.\n\n- **ORC-001 contract authority and limits: met.** The maintainer decision\n  above is the authority. The oracle's opening prose states the contract,\n  and Limits below states what it does not claim.\n- **ORC-002 independent judgment: met.** The model applies its existing drain\n  rule. Every ordinary source publication retires accepted snapshots, and a\n  source delete acknowledges no request. The model reads no production state\n  and imports no production classifier.\n- **ORC-003 distinguishable responsibilities: met.**\n  `optimistic-history-oracle.ts` keeps the contract prose, the\n  `HistoryModel`, the driver `runOptimisticHistory`, and its per-step refinement\n  check apart. The grammar lives in\n  `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts` as `sourceBatch` and\n  `optimisticHistory`.\n- **ORC-004 grammar controls: met, with one gap.**\n  - Reconstruction: the four pinned histories are legal histories of the\n    grammar. Edits and source deletes both draw keys from 1 to 3.\n  - Ablation and range: the fixed-campaign control requires absent-key\n    deletes, and it requires more deletes in total than absent-key deletes, so\n    held-key deletes remain.\n  - Exclusion: the driver no longer filters deletes. The source still writes\n    `insert` only for a key it lacks.\n  - Gap: no counter shows that the generator reaches an absent-key delete\n    while an accepted snapshot of the same key exists. The pinned histories\n    reach that premise directly.\n- **ORC-005 production path and observation: met.** The driver writes through\n  the real sync `begin`, `write`, and `commit` API of a Collection. After each\n  step it compares public rows, both subscribers' replicas, downstream query\n  rows, and request outcomes with the model.\n- **ORC-006 checker calibration: met.** A hostile mutant rejects a sync delete\n  for a key absent from the source projection. With the old grammar, the\n  optimistic-history campaigns pass under it. Only the separate retained\n  authoritative state property fails, and that property has no optimistic\n  layers. With the new grammar, the fixed and random optimistic-history\n  campaigns and the three accepted-insert histories fail. This ran on the\n  tree of `af0b8648a`. `74e5b80d6` adds only the fourth pinned history. A\n  mutant that drops any delete for a key absent from `syncedData` is too broad\n  to distinguish the change. The old grammar already kills it through queued\n  deletes of held keys.\n- **ORC-007 fixed and random campaigns: met.** The fixed campaign uses seed\n  86103. The random campaign runs under the property name\n  `collection-state.optimistic-history`, which accepts a seed and path for\n  replay. Both pass on `74e5b80d6`, and so do the 153 tests in the four files\n  that use the driver. On `af0b8648a`, the `@tanstack/db` suite passed 244\n  files and 8,269 tests.\n- **ORC-008 stateful-model minimality: not applicable.** The model gains no\n  state and loses none. An absent-key delete takes the existing delete branch\n  of the drain.\n- **ORC-009 vocabulary mapping: met, with a recorded term.** \"Accepted\n  snapshot\" maps to a completed direct mutation that the Collection retains\n  until source publication. The model's existing `retired` flag means that\n  the snapshot stopped overlaying the base. That differs from the glossary's\n  demand retirement. This change keeps the existing model term and does not\n  rename it.\n- **ORC-010 failure fidelity and cleanup: not applicable.** This change alters\n  no shrinking, capture, or cleanup path. The driver keeps its existing\n  `withHistoryCleanup`.\n- **ORC-011 independent second formulation: not applicable.** No reviewer\n  named a fault that production and the model could share for this case.\n- **ORC-012 review evidence: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013 distinguishing witness: met, with one limit.** The law is\n  conditional: an accepted snapshot retires, but an active request stays. The\n  accepted-insert histories reach the first side. The active-insert history is\n  the nearby witness for the second side. A wrong design that also removed the\n  active row would differ from the model at the step after the delete. No\n  mutant demonstrates that rejection.\n- **ORC-014 controlled-premise handoff: not applicable.** The oracle uses no\n  controlled provider or host. The source is the Collection's own sync API.\n\n## Limits\n\nAn immediate delete that arrives while the insert is active leaves the\naccepted row until the next source publication. That follows the existing\nrule that a delete acknowledges no request. Immediate sync batches are\nscheduled for removal in a separate change, so this record does not revisit\nthat rule.\n\n## Update, 2026-10-05: settlement-drop law\n\nThe record above describes the retention law that was current on\n`06cab6fc7`. The settlement-drop change replaces that law. See\n[the settlement-drop review](2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md). The following\nnow applies to this owner:\n\n- A source delete for a key the source never held removes no applied synced\n  row and acknowledges no request.\n- If the source commits the delete while the transaction persists, or inside\n  its handler, the delete is queued. The completed row is held, and the drop\n  and the delete publish together. The row is gone after settlement.\n- Once the optimistic state has dropped, the delete changes no visible row.\n- A persisting transaction keeps its optimistic row. This includes a truncate\n  that carries the delete: the truncate applies at once, beneath that row.\n- The `immediate` dimension is gone, because `begin({ immediate })` was\n  removed.\n\nThe pinned histories in `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts`\nnow cover these cases:\n\n- a queued delete;\n- a delete inside the handler;\n- a delete after the drop;\n- a persisting insert with a queued delete;\n- a persisting insert with a truncate delete.\n\nThe driver and its counters are unchanged.\n\nMutants on the merged branch:\n\n- **Reject (throw on) a delete for a key absent from the source projection:**\n  killed. The state-retention oracle fails 11 tests, including the fixed and\n  random campaigns and the pinned histories.\n- **Silently drop the same delete:** survived, and is equivalent under the\n  new law. The key is absent from the applied synced rows, and the\n  optimistic state drops at settlement whether or not a queued sync\n  transaction touches the key. So readers see the same rows and the same\n  publications. Under the retention law, this delete was what retired the\n  accepted snapshot, so the mutant was observable there.\n\nThe ORC-004 gap and the ORC-013 limit recorded above still apply.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Source delete for a key the source never held\n\nBase revision: `06cab6fc7` (`main` after #2005). Tested revision:\n`74e5b80d6`. Production code is unchanged from the base revision.\n\n## Contract\n\nA source may delete a key it does not hold. A typical cause: the backend\naccepted an optimistic insert, then deleted the row before the source\nstreamed it. The maintainer decision for this owner is:\n\n- An accepted optimistic snapshot of the key retires, as it does for any\n  ordinary source publication. The row disappears.\n- An active request for the key stays in place. The delete removes no base\n  row and acknowledges no request.\n\n`main` already behaves this way. This review adds the generated histories\nand witnesses. It changes no production code.\n\n## Gap\n\nThe coverage map listed the case as open. The optimistic-history driver\ndropped every delete for a key the source did not hold before the model or\nthe Collection saw it. So no optimistic-history campaign ever wrote such a\ndelete.\n\n## Witnesses\n\n- The driver keeps deletes for keys the source does not hold and counts them.\n  The fixed campaign must write at least one, beside deletes of held keys.\n- Three pinned histories in\n  `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts` accept an optimistic\n  insert and then delete its key from a source that never held it. They cover\n  a queued delete, an immediate delete, and a delete written inside the\n  insert handler.\n- A fourth pinned history writes an immediate delete while the insert is\n  still active. The row stays visible through settlement.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\nThe record claims a repaired generated-history grammar, so ORC-012 applies.\nEach requirement follows.\n\n- **ORC-001 contract authority and limits: met.** The maintainer decision\n  above is the authority. The oracle's opening prose states the contract,\n  and Limits below states what it does not claim.\n- **ORC-002 independent judgment: met.** The model applies its existing drain\n  rule. Every ordinary source publication retires accepted snapshots, and a\n  source delete acknowledges no request. The model reads no production state\n  and imports no production classifier.\n- **ORC-003 distinguishable responsibilities: met.**\n  `optimistic-history-oracle.ts` keeps the contract prose, the\n  `HistoryModel`, the driver `runOptimisticHistory`, and its per-step refinement\n  check apart. The grammar lives in\n  `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts` as `sourceBatch` and\n  `optimisticHistory`.\n- **ORC-004 grammar controls: met, with one gap.**\n  - Reconstruction: the four pinned histories are legal histories of the\n    grammar. Edits and source deletes both draw keys from 1 to 3.\n  - Ablation and range: the fixed-campaign control requires absent-key\n    deletes, and it requires more deletes in total than absent-key deletes, so\n    held-key deletes remain.\n  - Exclusion: the driver no longer filters deletes. The source still writes\n    `insert` only for a key it lacks.\n  - Gap: no counter shows that the generator reaches an absent-key delete\n    while an accepted snapshot of the same key exists. The pinned histories\n    reach that premise directly.\n- **ORC-005 production path and observation: met.** The driver writes through\n  the real sync `begin`, `write`, and `commit` API of a Collection. After each\n  step it compares public rows, both subscribers' replicas, downstream query\n  rows, and request outcomes with the model.\n- **ORC-006 checker calibration: met.** A hostile mutant rejects a sync delete\n  for a key absent from the source projection. With the old grammar, the\n  optimistic-history campaigns pass under it. Only the separate retained\n  authoritative state property fails, and that property has no optimistic\n  layers. With the new grammar, the fixed and random optimistic-history\n  campaigns and the three accepted-insert histories fail. This ran on the\n  tree of `af0b8648a`. `74e5b80d6` adds only the fourth pinned history. A\n  mutant that drops any delete for a key absent from `syncedData` is too broad\n  to distinguish the change. The old grammar already kills it through queued\n  deletes of held keys.\n- **ORC-007 fixed and random campaigns: met.** The fixed campaign uses seed\n  86103. The random campaign runs under the property name\n  `collection-state.optimistic-history`, which accepts a seed and path for\n  replay. Both pass on `74e5b80d6`, and so do the 153 tests in the four files\n  that use the driver. On `af0b8648a`, the `@tanstack/db` suite passed 244\n  files and 8,269 tests.\n- **ORC-008 stateful-model minimality: not applicable.** The model gains no\n  state and loses none. An absent-key delete takes the existing delete branch\n  of the drain.\n- **ORC-009 vocabulary mapping: met, with a recorded term.** \"Accepted\n  snapshot\" maps to a completed direct mutation that the Collection retains\n  until source publication. The model's existing `retired` flag means that\n  the snapshot stopped overlaying the base. That differs from the glossary's\n  demand retirement. This change keeps the existing model term and does not\n  rename it.\n- **ORC-010 failure fidelity and cleanup: not applicable.** This change alters\n  no shrinking, capture, or cleanup path. The driver keeps its existing\n  `withHistoryCleanup`.\n- **ORC-011 independent second formulation: not applicable.** No reviewer\n  named a fault that production and the model could share for this case.\n- **ORC-012 review evidence: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013 distinguishing witness: met, with one limit.** The law is\n  conditional: an accepted snapshot retires, but an active request stays. The\n  accepted-insert histories reach the first side. The active-insert history is\n  the nearby witness for the second side. A wrong design that also removed the\n  active row would differ from the model at the step after the delete. No\n  mutant demonstrates that rejection.\n- **ORC-014 controlled-premise handoff: not applicable.** The oracle uses no\n  controlled provider or host. The source is the Collection's own sync API.\n\n## Limits\n\nAn immediate delete that arrives while the insert is active leaves the\naccepted row until the next source publication. That follows the existing\nrule that a delete acknowledges no request. Immediate sync batches are\nscheduled for removal in a separate change, so this record does not revisit\nthat rule.\n\n## Update, 2026-10-05: settlement-drop law\n\nThe record above describes the retention law that was current on\n`06cab6fc7`. The settlement-drop change replaces that law. See\n[the settlement-drop review](2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md). The following\nnow applies to this owner:\n\n- A source delete for a key the source never held removes no applied synced\n  row and acknowledges no request.\n- If the source commits the delete while the transaction persists, or inside\n  its handler, the delete is queued. The completed row is held, and the drop\n  and the delete publish together. The row is gone after settlement.\n- Once the optimistic state has dropped, the delete changes no visible row.\n- A persisting transaction keeps its optimistic row. This includes a truncate\n  that carries the delete: the truncate applies at once, beneath that row.\n- The `immediate` dimension is gone, because `begin({ immediate })` was\n  removed.\n\nThe pinned histories in `collection-state-retention-oracle.property.test.ts`\nnow cover these cases:\n\n- a queued delete;\n- a delete inside the handler;\n- a delete after the drop;\n- a persisting insert with a queued delete;\n- a persisting insert with a truncate delete.\n\nThe driver and its counters are unchanged.\n\nMutants on the merged branch:\n\n- **Reject (throw on) a delete for a key absent from the source projection:**\n  killed. The state-retention oracle fails 11 tests, including the fixed and\n  random campaigns and the pinned histories.\n- **Silently drop the same delete:** survived, and is equivalent under the\n  new law. The key is absent from the applied synced rows, and the\n  optimistic state drops at settlement whether or not a queued sync\n  transaction touches the key. So readers see the same rows and the same\n  publications. Under the retention law, this delete was what retired the\n  accepted snapshot, so the mutant was observable there.\n\nThe ORC-004 gap and the ORC-013 limit recorded above still apply.\n"}
